      "sequence_position": 3,
      "problem": "Consider the set $A=[1,\\sqrt{5}].$ Complete the following reasoning to identify $sup(A).$\n\nThe supremum of a set $A$ is the {{field-1}} of $A.$\n\nThe set of upper bounds of $A$ is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{x\\in R\\,:\\,x{{field-2}}{{field-3}}\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, {{field-4}}.",
      "worked_solution": "The supremum of a set $A$ is the smallest upper bound of $A.$\n\nTo find the supremum of this set, we must find the set containing **all** upper bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of upper bounds) and take the minimum of this set.\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n- Any number in the set $x\\ge \\sqrt{5}$ is greater than or equal to the elements of $A.$ Thus, the set $[\\sqrt{5},\\infty )$ contains only upper bounds of $A.$\n\n- Any number in the set $x<\\sqrt{5}$ is **not** an upper bound of $A.$ In other words, we can always find an element of $A$ that is greater than $x$ for any $x<\\sqrt{5}.$\n\nTherefore, $[\\sqrt{5},\\infty )$ is the set of upper bounds of $A,$ and\n\n$$\nsup(A)=min\\,[\\sqrt{5},\\infty )=\\sqrt{5}.\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\sqrt{5}$ is also the maximum of $A.$\n\nThe completed reasoning is given below.\n\nThe supremum of $A$ is the $\\text{smallest upper bound}$ of $A.$\n\nThe set of upper bounds of $A$ is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\{x\\in R\\,:\\,x\\ge \\sqrt{5}\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $sup(A)=\\sqrt{5}\\in A.$",
